## Best Practices

- Use stable unique IDs and apply `update:items`. Rendering a fixed array while ignoring the emitted order intentionally keeps the caller order unchanged.
- Keep your own item objects and business actions. Listen to `select` for button actions; supply `href` for actual links.
- Provide localized labels and nonempty item names. Icon content should remain decorative, not a second nested button.
- Size icons at `1em`. The tile's font size follows the magnification; a fixed-pixel icon does not grow with it.
- Use `reorderable=false` for fixed app/navigation order. Disabled items remain part of the data but cannot activate, focus through the toolbar or start a drag.
- Keep the dock compact enough for its host; the room magnification needs is already inside the component. Visible labels truncate to their slot, so keep `showLabels=false` and use the name bubble when names run long.
- Do not add a second hover timer or spring. The component owns motion activity and cancels the single RAF when it settles or suspends.

## Technologies

The implementation uses native pointer capture and HTML toolbar semantics. Layout is measured on input events and order changes, never in spring frames: before an order change each item's old position is recorded, and after it the item springs from there into its new slot (FLIP). Pointer magnification measures icons where they are drawn; keyboard focus measures resting slots, so both sides match. `stepSpring` (`utils/animation/spring`) advances each axis on one of three springs. Size keeps the source parameters and never overshoots; making room is critically damped, about 0.3s; only a released icon inherits the pointer's velocity over the last 100ms, at a damping ratio near 0.8, so it may overshoot slightly. A single demand-driven RAF writes the size custom property, `translate` and `scale` of each item and stops once everything is at rest. `useMotionActivity` owns SSR-safe visibility, hidden-document, reduced-motion and KeepAlive suspension. On deactivation the icons go straight to rest; RAF ownership, pointer capture and pending drag state are released on deactivation/unmount. No observers or global listeners are duplicated by the dock.

